Thank you for your time and effort in reviewing our work, and the valuable comments. Point-to-point responses can be found below to each reviewer. We are also glad to improve our work to address any further concerns continually. Looking forward to furthering discussion with you.

**Q1: Datasets**

The implementation details of the three datasets satisfying the multi-task condition can be found in the supplementary material of [1] (see Algorithm 2, https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.06755). To avoid ambiguity, we give a detailed experimental setting.

In each run, we split the graphs into 70%, 10%, and 20% for training $B_{tra}$, validation $B_{val}$, and test $B_{test}$. For the node classification, we mimic the common semi-supervised setting, where feature vectors are available for all nodes, and only a small subset of nodes (30% in this paper) is labeled [2]. For the link prediction, in each graph, some randomly removed edges and unremoved edges are treated as positive examples. Couples of non-adjacent nodes are randomly sampled as negative examples. The steps are as follows:

for each graphs $g$ in datasets $B$ ($B_{tra}$, $B_{val}$, or $B_{test}$) do:

//Node Classification

num_labelled_nodes $\leftarrow$ $g$.num_nodes*30%;

$N$ $\leftarrow$ divide nodes per class, then iteratively randomly sample one node per class without replacement and add it to $N$ until $|N|$ = num_labelled_nodes;

$g$.labelled_nodes $\leftarrow$ $N$;

// Link Prediction

$E^N$ $\leftarrow$ randomly pick negative samples (edges that are not in the graph; possibly in the same number as the number of edges in the graph);

$E^P$ $\leftarrow$ randomly remove 20% of the edges in $g$;

 $g'$ $\leftarrow$ $g$ removed of $E^P$;

$g$.positive edges $\leftarrow$  $g'$.edges;

$g$.negative edges $\leftarrow$ $E^P$.

We will upload the revised version in the future (the manuscript cannot be edited in this phase). In addition, our tasks contain **both graph-level (graph classification) and node-level (node classification and link prediction) tasks**. Three standard datasets containing multiple graphs (ENZYMES, PROTEINS, and DHFR) are adopted. Therefore, our experimental setting is reasonable and rigorous. Our experiments fully verify the effectiveness of the proposed bi-level multi-objective optimization algorithm for automatic multi-task graph representation learning.

[1] Graph Representation Learning for Multi-Task Settings: a Meta-Learning Approach, IJCNN, 2022.

[2] Semi-Supervised Classification with Graph Convolutional Networks, ICLR, 2017.

**Q2: Standard Node Classification and Link Prediction Datasets**

Our tasks contain **both graph-level and node-level tasks**. Standard node classification and link prediction datasets, such as Cora, CiteSeer, and PubMed, often contain a single graph. Therefore, they do not satisfy the multi-task condition for graph-level tasks. In order to further dispel the reviewers' concerns, we added a set of comparative experiments for solving multiple node-level tasks (node classification NC, link prediction LP, and attribute inference AI) to illustrate the effectiveness of our method. Three widely used datasets (Cora, CiteSeer, and PubMed) are adopted. The comparison algorithms include a single-level multi-objective (multi-task handcrafted) baseline (JAME)[1]. In each run, we split the nodes into 60%, 20%, and 20% for training, validation, and testing. The other experimental settings are exactly the same as in [1]. The source code of JAME can be found in the original paper. The experimental results in the table below can still show the superiority of our method **when solving multiple node-level tasks**.

|   |  Cora-NC |  Cora-LP |  Cora-AI |  CiteSeer-NC |  CiteSeer-LP |  CiteSeer-AI |  PubMed-NC |  PubMed-LP |  PubMed-AI |
